  <h1>Spackle</h1>
  <p class="dim">Self-contained polyfills for older hardware. Measured against core-js 3.50, 2026-10-30. Spackle release 0.1.0-alpha.9.</p>

  <div class="abstract">
    <p><b>Abstract.</b> Polyfill libraries often route API calls through shared helpers. Spackle instead keeps each polyfill self-contained, reducing helper work and temporary allocations. In a simulated front-end workload across 24 Chromium WebViews (41 to 150), the oldest engines used up to 6× less polyfill time and produced up to 7× less garbage with Spackle. From Chromium 69, results were within 10%, as both libraries relied more on native implementations. On one 2017 LG TV, the SolidTV demo app's median initial render was 4.53 seconds with core-js and 1.03 seconds with Spackle (4.4× faster). These results describe the tested workload and device; they are not a guarantee for every application.</p>
  </div>


  <div class="paper">
    <h2 id="problem">1. The problem</h2>
    <p>Many devices ship WebViews years behind desktop browsers, often with slower CPUs and less available memory. Applications support those engines through a polyfill library (a common choice is core-js). It is built the way a library should be: every API routed through shared internals (<code>toObject</code>, <code>lengthOfArrayLike</code>, <code>getIteratorMethod</code>, and so on), each one correct, each one small. Most engineers learn DRY early, and it is usually the right call.</p>
    <p>DRY is costing us. For example, a single polyfilled <code>Array.from</code> walks a chain of nested helper functions, and several of those allocate: an iterator record, a bound function, an options object. On a modern machine this is a negligible slowdown. But on older, slower hardware (where these polyfills are needed), garbage collection runs on the main thread, which causes UI jitter and makes apps feel sluggish.</p>

    <h2 id="approach">2. Approach: islands</h2>
    <p>Each Spackle polyfill is one function with no imports. Instead of DRY, Spackle was built to be WET (write everything twice): polyfills that rely on other polyfills have those dependencies inlined.</p>
    <p>So there are no helper chains: the hot path is a single frame with the work in it and nothing else. Spackle also avoids temporary per-call allocations in its polyfill paths, reducing the short-lived objects the collector would otherwise need to clean up.</p>
    <p>The obvious objection is maintenance: a fix to shared logic has to be made in every island that carries it. In practice this has not been as much of a burden as expected. The functions are small by nature, and the duplicated part is a few lines of coercion.</p>
    <p>Shared helpers have the opposite problem: a change to one helper changes every API routed through it, so a fix for <code>Array.from</code> is also, silently, a change to <code>Map</code>, <code>Promise.all</code> and forty other entry points. An island's blast radius is the island.</p>
    <p>The trade is scope. core-js tracks the whole language and unfinished proposals, across Node and most browsers. Spackle focuses on established APIs, in browsers only, from Safari 7.1 and Chromium 38 up.</p>
    <p>As a direct test of the WET design: the same <code>Map</code> polyfill written twice, once as an island and once routed through shared helpers, with nothing else different. The island was 1.66× faster and produced 2.2× less garbage.</p>

    <h2 id="method">3. Method</h2>
    <p>Spackle was run against core-js in a WebView running each Chromium version from 41 to 150, about every fifth release, 24 versions in all. Every run had a fresh browser, a 64 MB JavaScript heap and one CPU core. Each test ran twice with the library order swapped; the median is reported. Garbage collection counts, pause times and heap sizes come from V8's own log, not from a sampling profiler.</p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th></tr>
      <tr><td>WebViews</td><td>24 Chromium versions, 41 to 150</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Heap</td><td>64 MB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CPU</td><td>Single core</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Passes</td><td>2,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Runs</td><td>2 per test, library order swapped, median reported</td></tr>
      <tr><td>GC measurement</td><td>V8's own log</td></tr>
      <tr><td>core-js entry</td><td><code>core-js/stable</code> and the full bundle: results within noise of each other</td></tr>
    </table></div>
    <p><b>The stress test</b> is a render loop that calls polyfilled built-ins the way a front-end framework does on each render: <code>Object.assign</code>, <code>Array.from</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>URL</code>, string and array helpers, hundreds of calls per pass. It is deliberately a worst case for a polyfill library. It measures what the polyfills cost, not whole-application speed.</p>
    <p><b>The hot-path test</b> calls one API 500 times in a row and times it, after a warm-up, averaged over 10 executions.</p>

    <h2 id="results">4. Results</h2>
    <h3>Stress test, by Chromium version</h3>
    <p class="dim">Spackle relative to core-js. ≈ means within 10%.</p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <tr><th>Chromium</th><th class="c">41</th><th class="c">45</th><th class="c">50</th><th class="c">56</th><th class="c">61</th><th class="c">69+</th></tr>
