    </table></div>
    <h3>Stress test on the TV</h3>
    <p class="dim">The same stress test as section 3, cut to 500 passes, the most the TV can run. Three runs.</p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th class="num">Spackle vs core-js</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Garbage</b></td><td class="num"><b>~9× less</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Garbage collections</b></td><td class="num"><b>~5× fewer</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Peak heap</b></td><td class="num"><b>37% lower</b></td></tr>
    </table></div>

    <h2 id="compliance">6. Spec compliance</h2>
    <p>Behavior was checked with 1,357 selected checks derived from test262 and web-platform-tests, run in the browser with each library installed over the engine's own natives. This is a targeted check set covering each polyfill Spackle supports. Some checks are skipped where a polyfill crashes the WebView (this happens with core-js). Spackle aims to match core-js compliance. In the recorded runs (Chrome 38–39 and Safari 7.1–11 on real browsers, every Chromium 41–150 on a custom emulator), there is no check core-js passes that Spackle fails, and Spackle fails fewer checks on every engine. An important note: core-js is not fully spec compliant either. On Safari 7.1 it fails 125 checks and freezes the page on 4; Spackle fails 24. The per-engine table and the same 1,357 checks for your browser are on <a href="/verify">Verify</a>.</p>

